---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Src from '../components/Src.astro';
const title = 'How to get an apostille in North Carolina';
const description = 'North Carolina apostille, authentication, and authority certificates: the $10 fee, the Raleigh mailing and walk-in addresses, the wet-signature translation rule, school-document notarization, and the post-April-2000 signature rule.';
const HOME = 'https://www.sosnc.gov/divisions/authentications';
const APO = 'https://www.sosnc.gov/divisions/authentications/Apostille_Certificates';
const FEES = 'https://www.sosnc.gov/fees/by_title/_authentications';
const FAQ = 'https://www.sosnc.gov/frequently_asked_questions/by_title/_Authentications';
const TAT = 'https://www.sosnc.gov/divisions/authentications/TurnAround_Times';
const COURIER = 'https://www.sosnc.gov/divisions/authentications/Courier_Policies';
const FORMS = 'https://www.sosnc.gov/forms/by_title/_Authentications';
---
<Base title={title} description={description} updated="2026-11-23">
  <h1>How to get an apostille in North Carolina</h1>
  <p class="lede">The North Carolina Secretary of State’s Authentication Office in Raleigh issues apostilles, authentications, and certificates for North Carolina documents, including birth and marriage certificates, statements of marital status, corporate documents, powers of attorney, diplomas, school transcripts, and deeds.<Src href={HOME} /></p>

  <h2>At a glance</h2>
  <div class="tablewrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Item</th><th>What the official page says</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Fee</td><td>$10.00 per document. For adoptions, a duplicate original requested at the same time is $5.00; requested later, $10.00. Checks or money orders payable to “North Carolina Secretary of State.”<Src href={FEES} /><Src href={APO} /></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Refunds</td><td>The $10 fee may be refunded if the document is rejected, not processed, and you request a refund.<Src href={APO} /></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Mailing address</td><td>North Carolina Secretary of State, Authentication Office, Post Office Box 29622, Raleigh, North Carolina 27626-0622<Src href={HOME} /></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Walk-in address</td><td>North Carolina Secretary of State, Authentication Office, 2 South Salisbury Street, Raleigh, North Carolina 27601-2903. Phone 919-814-5400. Office hours Monday to Friday, 8:00 a.m. to 5:00 p.m.<Src href={HOME} /></td></tr>
        <tr><td>In person</td><td>No appointment needed. Hand-delivered documents are generally processed more quickly than mailed ones.<Src href={TAT} /></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Mail processing</td><td>Mailed documents generally take five business days longer than hand-delivered documents. The office posts current turnaround times on its authentications home page.<Src href={TAT} /></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Online filing</td><td>None. The office says it does not do online authentications.<Src href={FAQ} /></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>Three kinds of certificate</h2>
  <p>North Carolina issues three different certificates, and which one you get depends on the destination country and the kind of document:<Src href={FAQ} /></p>
  <div class="tablewrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Certificate</th><th>Used for</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Apostille</td><td>Documents going to countries that have signed the Hague Convention.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Authentication</td><td>North Carolina government-issued documents going to countries that have not signed the Hague Convention.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Authority</td><td>Documents notarized in North Carolina going to countries that have not signed the Hague Convention.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>Once you have an apostille, the document can go directly to the destination country without further certification from the U.S. Department of State.<Src href={APO} /> For how the two systems differ, see <a href="/apostille-vs-authentication/">apostille versus authentication</a>.</p>

  <h2>Step by step</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Make sure it is in English.</strong> If the document is in another language, get a certified or notarized English translation.<Src href={APO} /> Since late 2024, the English translation must carry a wet (original) signature.<Src href={FAQ} /></li>
    <li><strong>Get the right signature on it.</strong> Sign before a North Carolina notary, or get a certified copy notarized by the issuing entity. For a copy of a North Carolina document, you can use a notarized “true copy” affidavit; the office provides a sample on its cover letters and templates page.<Src href={APO} /></li>
    <li><strong>Prepare the cover letter.</strong> It needs a contact name, a daytime phone number, the country where the document will be used, and the correct fee.<Src href={FAQ} /> The cover letter is on the office’s forms page.<Src href={FORMS} /></li>
    <li><strong>Send or deliver it.</strong> Include the original documents with all certifications attached, the payment, and a prepaid return envelope or carrier label.<Src href={APO} /></li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Documents that don’t need a notary</h2>
  <p>Documents from these North Carolina offices go straight to the Authentication Office without a notarization:<Src href={APO} /></p>
  <ul>
    <li>Register of Deeds</li>
    <li>Clerk of Superior Court</li>
    <li>North Carolina Department of Agriculture</li>
    <li>North Carolina Department of Health and Human Services</li>
    <li>North Carolina Vital Records (phone 919-733-3526)</li>
    <li>North Carolina Department of Motor Vehicles (Raleigh office only)</li>
    <li>North Carolina Attorney General’s Office</li>
    <li>North Carolina Legislative Services</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Diplomas and transcripts</h2>
  <p>Transcripts, report cards, diplomas, and similar documents from a North Carolina university, college, community college, or high school must be notarized by a North Carolina notary public. A school seal applied by the registrar is not acceptable.<Src href={APO} /></p>

  <h2>Rules that cause rejections</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Old signatures.</strong> The certifying official’s signature must be from April 2000 or later, because the office does not have official signatures on record from before 2000.<Src href={FAQ} /><Src href={APO} /></li>
    <li><strong>Out-of-state documents.</strong> North Carolina can’t authenticate a document from another state.<Src href={FAQ} /></li>
    <li><strong>Missing translation.</strong> A foreign-language document without a certified or notarized English translation is denied.<Src href={FAQ} /></li>
    <li><strong>U.S. use.</strong> Documents to be used in the United States can’t be authenticated.<Src href={FAQ} /></li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Return shipping</h2>
  <p>Without a prepaid label or self-addressed stamped envelope, documents come back by regular mail. A prepaid courier label must be computer-printed and paid for when you print it; a handwritten label with an account number doesn’t count. The office rejects FedEx labels that list the Secretary of State with “Bill Sender.” UPS and FedEx air labels are picked up automatically; for ground labels, the office calls you and you schedule the pickup.<Src href={COURIER} /><Src href={APO} /></p>
  <p>For documents going back to a foreign address, the office no longer uses UPS, FedEx, or DHL. It will mail a USPS prepaid envelope and label to a foreign address as long as the sender address isn’t the Secretary of State’s.<Src href={COURIER} /></p>
  <p>Emergencies can be considered for expedited processing if you send documentation of the emergency.<Src href={APO} /></p>

